WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:05.029
The satirical horror movie Velvet Buzzsaw
explores what happens when the pretentious

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:09.920
world of modern art collides with a collection
of supernaturally-charged paintings from a

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.110
dead artist with a dark and horrific past.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:16.360
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm explaining everything you need

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.949
to know about Velvet Buzzsaw, the movie's
ending and what each of the kills represents.

00:00:20.949 --> 00:00:25.509
Plus I'll be delving into who Vetril Dease
really is and how his supernatural art comes

00:00:25.509 --> 00:00:26.650
to life.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:30.929
As you'd expect, spoilers ahead for the movie,
so take care if you haven't seen it yet.

00:00:30.929 --> 00:00:35.440
When ambitious assistant Josephina stumbles
across the dark, creepy art of dead painter

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.820
Vetril Dease, her decision to commercialise
the work that the painter wanted to destroy

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.000
kicks off a series of escalating horrific
deaths.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.870
As Josephina looks at one of his portraits,
the ashes of paintings that Dease was burning

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:52.690
before he died, re-ignite, the first indication
of the supernatural forces inside his art.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:57.110
The eyes on this portrait also seem to shift
slightly from the centre to the side looking

00:00:57.110 --> 00:01:02.610
at Josephina as she unravels and examines
more paintings inside the dead artist's apartment.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:06.939
Those watching eyes are a spine-chilling premonition
of what's to come for many of the characters

00:01:06.939 --> 00:01:08.039
in the movie.

00:01:08.039 --> 00:01:12.320
Eyes often show up shortly before a character
dies, as if they're observing and making a

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.929
moral judgement.

00:01:13.929 --> 00:01:18.030
Just after gallery owner Rhodora reveals to
Josephina that she's deliberately removing

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:22.880
many of Dease's works from the market in order
to create scarcity and drive up the value,

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.469
the eyes on a wall watch Bryson who's been
tasked with hiding the art and who opens up

00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:31.049
a crate removing some of the paintings presumably
to keep for himself.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:36.680
Bryson, of course, ends up dead shortly after
at the hands of some painted monkey mechanics.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.609
Just before Gretchen's deadly encounter with
the sphere, the blurred eyes of the boy and

00:01:40.609 --> 00:01:43.159
girl in this portrait come into focus.

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:47.469
It's as if Dease's spirit is awakening in
the painting and is about to pass deadly judgement

00:01:47.469 --> 00:01:52.200
on Gretchen for how she's abandoned the principles
of her former position as a museum curator

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.799
to make money and deliberately gain special
access and favours for private clients.

00:01:56.799 --> 00:02:01.630
Likewise, the eyes in a sinister doll open
up to watch gallery owner Jon just before

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.630
he's strangled by an installation in his gallery.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:08.759
And after Josephina ditches Damrish when he
decides to go back to his art collective,

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:13.170
there's an especially creepy eye on the graffitied
wall behind her looking right at her.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:18.299
The mural comes alive setting a spectacular
trap for her as a gallery full of graffiti

00:02:18.299 --> 00:02:20.860
art opens up behind the wall.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:25.370
Josephina's death seems particularly appropriate
as the paint from the art that she so despises

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.180
melts, runs towards her and then consumes
her.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:32.670
And, after she's killed we see she's been
incorporated directly into the graffitied

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:33.740
wall.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.980
What's going on with each death is that each
victim ends up becoming part of the deadly

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:38.980
artwork.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.599
Bryson was pulled into the painting of the
monkey mechanics, and the sphere consumed

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:47.129
Gretchen's arm and her dead body was assumed
by most people to be just another part of

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:49.069
the art installation.

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:52.030
Remember that the archivist who was studying
Dease's paintings for Rhodora discovered there

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:55.080
was blood tissue in every one of the works
she studied.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.159
She assumed the blood belonged to Dease, and
that seems quite plausible because in one

00:02:59.159 --> 00:03:03.030
of the flashbacks, there's a bowl full of
blood with a bloodied knife and razor next

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:07.069
to it, and we also see a glimpse of Dease
painting with a hand partially wrapped in

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:11.969
a bandage of sorts and what looks like cuts
or wounds around his wrists.

00:03:11.969 --> 00:03:15.260
Writer-director Dan Gilroy has talked about
how one of the themes of Velvet Buzzsaw is

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:18.849
"the idea that artists invest their spirit
into their art."

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:23.640
It seems as if by literally painting his own
flesh and blood into his works, Dease has

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.909
invested them with a supernatural spirit.

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:30.000
While the characters in the film are all desperately
trying to take advantage of Dease's artwork

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.109
and exploit his life and everything he lived
through for their own gain, what they don't

00:03:34.109 --> 00:03:38.739
realise, as Daveed Diggs, who plays Damrish,
has said, "is that Dease's past and the violence

00:03:38.739 --> 00:03:41.340
he lived will actually reach out and kill
you."

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.120
If new victims like Josephina and Bryson end
up becoming part of the art which killed them,

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.939
then perhaps it's also possible that some
of Dease's paintings contain blood tissue

00:03:49.939 --> 00:03:51.510
from his previous victims.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.999
For example we learn in the movie that as
an adult Dease tracked his abusive father,

00:03:55.999 --> 00:04:00.299
tortured him and burned him alive, but what
if Dease painted parts of his father into

00:04:00.299 --> 00:04:02.040
some of his paintings as well?

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.299
Now, we need to talk about Rhodora and the
importance of her tattoo "Velvet Buzzsaw"

00:04:06.299 --> 00:04:09.420
which carries a lot of meaning especially
as it's also the movie's title.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:14.450
First of all, it's the name of the punk rock
duo Rhodora formed with her friend Polly Anna.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:18.519
Damrish tells Rhodora he was a fan of their
early work, but the pair eventually split

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:19.519
up.

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:23.310
What matters though is that Rhodora abandoned
her original passion and anarchic roots and

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:28.120
decided instead to become part of the status
quo, building a career as a well-known dealer

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.190
in the moneyed and powerful world of art.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:35.009
As she's built her status in the art world,
Rhodora's learned how to trick and manipulate

00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:37.310
artists, buyers and rivals alike.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:41.500
She understands how to pick up promising new
talent like Damrish for her gallery while

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.800
simultaneously offloading talent like Piers
who's no longer productive onto her rival

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:46.800
Dondon.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:52.080
Rhodora also manoeuvres Josephina into partnering
with her to sell the stolen Dease paintings,

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.050
and in the past she's manipulated Morf behind
his back by using his boyfriend for inside

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:58.050
information.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:03.330
Her smarts almost seem to get her through
to the end of the film unscathed as she realises

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:08.810
just how dangerous Dease's paintings really
are and she empties her house of any art or

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:09.810
images.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.990
However, Dease's spirit still finds a way
to enact revenge on her for stealing his work,

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:18.419
and the "Velvet Buzzsaw" tattoo on her neck
springs to life slicing her to death while

00:05:18.419 --> 00:05:22.660
she's in a pose which mirrors the art previously
on her bedroom wall.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:27.560
Ultimately, as Rhodora herself said, "art
is always dangerous"; and the "no death, no

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:32.380
art" tattoo on her arm is, of course, particularly
fitting for both her and the movie.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:37.200
The name "Velvet Buzzsaw" is also an interesting
oxymoron as it implies something soft and

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.960
luxurious but sharp and deadly at the same
time.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.270
It applies to Rhodora and how she softly,
softly manipulates people around her while

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:49.169
also using more cutting methods to get what
she wants, for example, threats of legal action

00:05:49.169 --> 00:05:53.690
to force Josephina into sharing ownership
of Dease's art; and remember that Damrish

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:57.020
says he was specifically warned to be careful
of Rhodora.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:01.460
The name Velvet Buzzsaw also applies to the
lethal threat that a seemingly innocent piece

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:03.349
of art can pose.

00:06:03.349 --> 00:06:07.680
Velvet Buzzsaw is clearly a pointed critique
of the contemporary art world and the pretentious

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:13.039
critic Morf certainly isn't spared as he ends
up dead at the hands of Hobo Man, the walking,

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:17.419
talking homeless superhero that Morf was so
willing to critically tear apart at the beginning

00:06:17.419 --> 00:06:18.419
of the movie.

00:06:18.419 --> 00:06:23.050
I wonder whether the inclusion of this particular
artwork of a down-on-his-luck, former superhero

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:27.599
is not only a comment on the changing nature
of what's popular or in when it comes to the

00:06:27.599 --> 00:06:31.240
art world, but also a reference to how one
of the most popular genres in current film

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:35.979
and TV entertainment, the superhero franchise,
will eventually, like other genres before

00:06:35.979 --> 00:06:38.970
it, fall out of the great favour it's currently
in.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:43.300
Gilroy also appears to think that critics
can be too fast to point out faults and criticise

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:46.490
the work an artist may have poured their heart
and soul into.

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:50.669
There's also some interesting commentary on
whether too much value is given to the opinion

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:51.669
of a critic.

00:06:51.669 --> 00:06:55.800
In the film, Rhodora says Morf "is a god"
in their world, and when he decides to publish

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.729
his negative review of Hobo Man, it kills
the sale of the piece that was brokered before

00:06:59.729 --> 00:07:00.729
the review ran.

00:07:00.729 --> 00:07:05.050
Morf is occasionally presented a little more
positively than some of the other characters,

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:10.720
such as when he turns down Gretchen's proposal
to feed her early information about his reviews.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:15.319
However, he demonstrates he's more than willing
to sacrifice any principles he may have if

00:07:15.319 --> 00:07:19.539
it's expedient for him to do so or to cater
to his own prejudices.

00:07:19.539 --> 00:07:23.950
For example, he gives a negative review to
Ricky's art show even though he liked it,

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:27.849
because he was sleeping with Josephina at
the time and she asked him to make her ex-boyfriend

00:07:27.849 --> 00:07:29.300
Ricky suffer.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:34.000
And when Morf discovers Damrish is sleeping
with Josephina, he yells "The admiration I

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.780
had for your work has completely evaporated!"

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:42.041
He's also ridiculously fussy and snobbish,
complaining about cheesy funeral music; and

00:07:42.041 --> 00:07:46.990
although he warns Rhodora that Dease's work
is dangerous and to "stop selling it, box

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:50.539
it up and get rid of it", we see that he's
kept some of it for himself and is going to

00:07:50.539 --> 00:07:55.220
lock it away in storage, presumably to sell
on in the future for a huge profit.

00:07:55.220 --> 00:07:59.569
That moment is, of course, his ultimate undoing
because that's when he comes face to face

00:07:59.569 --> 00:08:04.259
with Hobo Man who he disparaged so savagely
at the beginning of the film.

00:08:04.259 --> 00:08:08.030
Ultimately then, the characters who die in
Velvet Buzzsaw receive a death that parallels

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:09.360
how they've behaved.

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:14.410
Morf is literally torn apart by the thing
which he figuratively tore to shreds earlier,

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:18.900
and as the movie's producer Jennifer Fox says,
Josephina "is consumed by the graffiti art

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:23.830
she rejected and Rhodora by selling out her
punk roots […] Bryson is killed by acting

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:27.319
on his primitive nature [and] Gretchen for
over reaching."

00:08:27.319 --> 00:08:32.360
So, why are some characters not targeted by
Dease's supernatural creations and survive

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:33.729
to the end of the movie?

00:08:33.729 --> 00:08:38.650
It seems that Gilroy may view Coco, Damrish
and Piers as relatively innocent in comparison

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:43.120
to the others and therefore perhaps worthy
of being spared the wrath of Dease.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:48.010
Coco, played by Natalia Dyer who many of you'll
know from Stranger Things, is too new to the

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:49.720
art scene to have been completely corrupted.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:54.370
As an outsider who's only just joining that
world, she's still clear-headed enough to

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:56.300
understand what isn't art.

00:08:56.300 --> 00:09:01.110
For example, after Gretchen bleeds to death
from having her arm sliced off by the Sphere,

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:06.140
Coco's the only person who can recognise that
a dead body sprawled on the floor isn't actually

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:07.510
a piece of art.

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:12.490
As artists themselves, John Malkovich's Piers
and Daveed Diggs's Damrish seem to understand

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:15.050
the underlying power of Dease's art.

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:19.200
Piers and Damrish both become mesmerised by
Dease's paintings and this seems to be the

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:24.270
trigger that leads Damrish to abandon Rhodora's
gallery and go back to his Collective, much

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:26.040
to Josephina's disgust.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:31.010
Piers also goes back to creating art purely
for himself, making sand drawings that the

00:09:31.010 --> 00:09:34.140
tide gradually washes away as the credits
roll.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:39.130
Interestingly enough, that scene was inspired
by Dan Gilroy's own experiences in the 1990s

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:43.560
writing the movie Superman Lives, starring
Nic Cage and directed by Tim Burton.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:48.950
When he found out the movie was being canned
after 18 months of work, Gilroy told EW that

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:53.170
he went down to the beach and thought about
how nothing he wrote was ever going to be

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:57.000
seen and so he "might as well come down and
write words in the sand and have the waves

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.630
just wash them away."

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:02.760
But that was also the day that Gilroy decided
to value what he creates not for how many

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:06.620
people see it or its commercial success, but
simply for himself.

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:11.350
However you feel about the movie, Velvet Buzzsaw
is more than just a horror film set in the

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:12.660
contemporary art world.

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:17.430
It's also a commentary about our relationship
with any art form, including movies and TV.

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:21.500
Velvet Buzzsaw's producer Fox has said it's
"about the commodification of art […] and

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:25.940
the way in which this self-selecting group
of curators and dealers […] is anointing

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:30.329
certain people, ignoring other people, and
[...] creating a false economy."

00:10:30.329 --> 00:10:35.790
Gilroy has also said "art can't really exist
without somebody paying for it, but once art

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:37.740
becomes monetized, it loses something.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:42.670
[And] the problem when you over-monetize 'Art,'
it becomes an object rather than something

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:45.769
imbued with a spirit, or an idea, or even
a soul."

00:10:45.769 --> 00:10:49.360
Which is why there's that line in the movie
where Josephina says "what's the point of

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:51.160
art if nobody sees it?"

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:55.910
The film itself suggests that it's only the
artists who realise the true value of art,

00:10:55.910 --> 00:11:01.769
as both Damrish and Piers reject the over-commoditised
art scene and return to their artistic roots.

00:11:01.769 --> 00:11:06.459
This whole idea of the point of art and who
sees it is also interesting because although

00:11:06.459 --> 00:11:11.380
Velvet Buzzsaw premiered at the Utah-based
indie film festival Sundance, the movie is

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:15.780
available on the global streaming service
Netflix, which means a lot more people will

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:19.480
likely see this film than if it were only
released in cinemas.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:23.420
As for writer-director Dan Gilroy, he has
various ambitions for the film: obviously,

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:27.590
he wants people to see and enjoy the movie,
but he also wants it to spark conversations

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:28.769
and ideas.

00:11:28.769 --> 00:11:32.850
On top of that, he told Vanity Fair that he
hopes "people look at art in a slightly different

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:37.560
way. [... and that they] realize the artists
have invested [...] their creative soul into

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:38.560
the work."

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:42.779
And finally, he'd like the movie "to do for
the art world what Jaws did for swimming."

00:11:42.779 --> 00:11:47.820
In other words, "When walking into a gallery
of art, one should fear what might happen..."

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:51.850
After all, what about all those Dease paintings
that have found their way into the hands of

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:54.779
street vendors selling them for five bucks
a piece?

00:11:54.779 --> 00:11:58.380
Will Dease be ok with this as the works are
in the hands of the people now rather than

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:00.060
in the privileged art scene?

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:03.820
Or will he move on from taking revenge on
the hypocritical art world to judging the

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:06.270
everyday hypocrisy of ordinary people?

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:08.660
So, what do you think of Velvet Buzzsaw?

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:10.519
And what did you take away from the movie?

00:12:10.519 --> 00:12:12.380
Let me know in the comments below.

